        [ ] I think one thing we can do is less backtracking?.. so like, if
            we're parsing `int x` and we don't yet know whether it's gonna
            be a declaration or an expression, I believe once we see `int`
            we know we must be in a declaration, so we don't have to check
            more patterns after that.
            So, we can give a better error message if we discover something
            unexpected once we're "locked in" to parsing a declaration, etc.
            And we could add an explicit thing for this to the grammar rule
            syntax, like `!lockin`, orrrr can we automatically detect it?..
            Consider:

                unary_expression
                    | sizeof_type:  'sizeof' '(' type_name ')'
                    | sizeof_expr:  'sizeof' unary_expression
                    | inc: '++' unary_expression
                    | dec: '--' unary_expression
                    | unary_operator cast_expression
                    | postfix_expression
                    ;

            ...once we see `sizeof`, it's clear (to me) that we must be in
            one of the first two patterns.
            But I guess in order to automatically know that, we would have
            to look inside unary_operator and postfix_expression to see
            whether they can ever start with `sizeof`.
            Hmmmmm.

